export const A1={title:'The PA profession and the Medical Case Challenge',hook:'In 1965, Dr. Eugene Stead at Duke University enrolled four former Navy hospital corpsmen in a new program. They already had battlefield medical experience; he gave them the medical knowledge to practice alongside physicians. Today more than 170,000 physician assistants diagnose illness, order tests, prescribe medicine and assist in surgery. This new HOSA event asks a team of two to think like PAs.',objectives:['Describe the Medical Case Challenge format, test plan and Round Two rating sheet.','Trace the history, education and certification of physician assistants.','Explain how PAs collaborate with physicians and the healthcare team.','Plan a five-minute team case presentation that hits every rating-sheet item.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'The Physician Assistant – Medical Case Challenge is a **team event for two competitors**. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**; the team’s **average** score decides which teams advance. The test plan: disease process and terminology including cancer **10%**; body systems—integumentary **4%**, musculoskeletal **6%**, circulatory and lymphatic **10%**, respiratory **6%**, digestive **10%**, endocrine **6%**, nervous and senses **8%**, reproductive **4%**, urinary **4%**, mental disorders **6%**; **patient assessment 12%**; and **diagnosis 14%**. The official references are *Current Medical Diagnosis & Treatment (CMDT) 2026* and **MedlinePlus**.',
'In **Round Two**, the team receives supporting documents for a complex medical case and has **15 minutes** to review it—using a textbook and identified resources—and then **5 minutes** to present to judges using only their notes. A timekeeper calls one minute remaining and stops the presentation at five minutes. **Both members must take an active role.** The final score is the team’s test average plus the presentation score (115 points); ties go to the higher test average.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Diagnosis','14%'],['Patient assessment','12%'],['Disease process and terminology (incl. cancer)','10%'],['Circulatory and lymphatic','10%'],['Digestive','10%'],['Nervous system and senses','8%'],['Musculoskeletal, respiratory, endocrine, mental disorders','6% each'],['Integumentary, reproductive, urinary','4% each']]}]],
['History and education of PAs',[
'The PA profession began in the 1960s, when the United States faced a **shortage of primary care physicians** and many military medics returned home with valuable skills. The first class graduated from **Duke University in 1967**. The **National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants (NCCPA)** was created in 1975, and the **ARC-PA** accredits programs. The **PA Education Association (PAEA)**, which sponsors this HOSA event, represents PA programs.',
'Most PA programs are **master’s degree** programs lasting about **27 months**, with classroom study followed by clinical rotations in family medicine, internal medicine, surgery, emergency medicine, pediatrics, women’s health and behavioral health. Applicants usually need a bachelor’s degree, science prerequisites and **patient care experience**. Graduates must pass the **PANCE** (Physician Assistant National Certifying Exam) to use the credential **PA-C** and obtain a state license. To stay certified, PAs earn **100 continuing medical education hours every two years** and take a recertification exam every ten years.',
order('Path to becoming a PA',['Bachelor’s degree with science prerequisites','Hands-on patient care experience','Accredited PA master’s program (about 27 months)','Clinical rotations in many specialties','Pass the PANCE to become PA-C','State license; ongoing CME and recertification'])]],
['What PAs do',[
'PAs **take medical histories**, **perform physical exams**, **order and interpret tests**, **diagnose** and **treat** illnesses, **prescribe medications** (in all 50 states), **counsel** patients on prevention, **perform procedures** and **assist in surgery**. They practice in every specialty and setting—primary care, emergency medicine, surgery, dermatology, orthopedics, psychiatry and more—and many work in rural and underserved communities. The profession is one of the fastest-growing in the country.',
'PAs practice in **collaboration** with physicians and other team members; the exact supervision or collaboration requirements depend on **state law** and the employer. Their generalist training allows PAs to **change specialties** without new certification. Strong communication, teamwork, clinical reasoning and lifelong learning are core professional traits.',
sort('Within typical PA practice?',['Typical PA role','Not a PA role'],[['Taking a history and performing an exam',0],['Ordering and interpreting a chest X-ray',0],['Prescribing an antibiotic',0],['Assisting in surgery',0],['Practicing without any license',1],['Certifying their own exam without the PANCE',1]])]],
['The Round Two rating sheet',[
'**Content (70 points):** **patient information** (15)—objective and subjective data, demographics, **chief complaint**, **past medical history**, **social and family history**; **clinical course** (15)—the patient’s condition and disease progression **in chronological order**; **accuracy of diagnosis** (20)—a correct diagnosis earns full points, and a reasonable **differential** diagnosis earns partial credit; and **treatment plan** (20)—**all aspects** of treatment.',
'**Organization (20 points):** a concise, thorough presentation with all key points (10) and a **summary** of all significant points (10). **Delivery (25 points):** voice (5), stage presence (5), diction, pronunciation and grammar with no vocal fillers (5), and **team participation** (10)—both members carry roughly equal parts. A complete treatment plan usually covers **medications**, **procedures or surgery**, **monitoring and follow-up**, **referrals**, **patient education**, **lifestyle changes** and **prevention**.',
match('Rating-sheet item and points',[['Patient information','15 points'],['Clinical course (chronological)','15 points'],['Accuracy of diagnosis','20 points'],['Treatment plan','20 points'],['Organization','10 points'],['Summary','10 points'],['Team participation','10 points'],['Voice, stage presence, diction','5 points each']])]],
['Planning the 15 minutes and the 5 minutes',[
'Split the review. One partner builds a **timeline** of the clinical course (dates, symptoms, test results, treatments); the other extracts **patient information** and begins the **differential diagnosis**. Meet after about 8 minutes to agree on the diagnosis, then build the treatment plan together and decide who presents what. Write notes in a clear outline—only your notes may be used during the presentation.',
'A balanced five-minute structure: **Partner A** (about 2½ minutes)—introduction, patient information and clinical course; **Partner B** (about 2½ minutes)—diagnosis with key supporting evidence, the full treatment plan and the summary. Speak to the judges, use medical terms correctly and define them briefly, avoid fillers and finish on time. Practice with a timer until you can deliver the structure smoothly.',
order('Presentation outline',['Introduce the team and the patient','Patient information: demographics, chief complaint, history','Clinical course in chronological order','Diagnosis and the evidence that supports it','Treatment plan: medications, procedures, monitoring, referrals, education, prevention','Summary of the key points']),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Partial credit for diagnosis',text:'If the team names a reasonable differential diagnosis but misses the exact diagnosis, judges may award partial points. State your leading diagnosis clearly and briefly explain the evidence, mentioning one or two alternatives you ruled out.'}]],
['Practice: first look at a case',[
kase('Dividing the work',{a:{text:'Your team opens a 12-page case file with 15 minutes on the clock. What is the best plan?',choices:[['Split the file: one builds the timeline, the other gathers history and starts the differential, then regroup','b','Correct. Parallel work saves time.',true],['Both read every page together from the start','b','You may run out of time.',false]]},b:{text:'With 4 minutes left, you agree on the diagnosis but have no treatment plan yet. What do you prioritize?',choices:[['A complete treatment plan—medications, monitoring, referrals, education and prevention','c','Correct. The treatment plan is worth 20 points.',true],['Rewriting the timeline neatly','c','The plan earns more points now.',false]]},c:{text:'You split the presentation evenly and finish with a clear summary at 4:50.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Two-person team; 50-question test (team average) across body systems, assessment 12% and diagnosis 14%; Round Two is a 15-minute case review and a 5-minute presentation scored out of 115.','PAs began at Duke in 1965–67; master’s programs of about 27 months, the PANCE, PA-C, state licensure, 100 CME hours every 2 years; PAs diagnose, treat and prescribe in collaboration with physicians.','Present patient information, a chronological clinical course, an accurate diagnosis and a complete treatment plan, then summarize, with both partners sharing equally.'],terms:[['PANCE','The national certifying exam for physician assistants.'],['Chief complaint','The main reason the patient is seeking care, in the patient’s words.'],['Differential diagnosis','A list of possible conditions that could explain the findings.']],mcqs:[q('How many competitors are on a Medical Case Challenge team?','Two','One','Three','Four','A team event.'),q('How long does the team have to review the case in Round Two?','15 minutes','5 minutes','60 minutes','30 minutes','Then 5 minutes to present.'),q('Where was the first PA program founded?','Duke University','Harvard University','Johns Hopkins','Stanford','By Dr. Eugene Stead.'),q('Which exam must PA graduates pass?','PANCE','NCLEX','NPTE','MCAT','Then they use PA-C.'),q('Which test-plan area has the highest weight?','Diagnosis','Urinary','Reproductive','Integumentary','14%.'),q('Which item is worth the most points on the presentation rating sheet?','Accuracy of diagnosis and treatment plan (20 each)','Voice','Stage presence','Summary','20 points each.'),q('What may teams use during the 5-minute presentation?','Their notes from the case review','The textbook','A slideshow','Phones','Notes only.'),q('How often must PAs complete 100 CME hours?','Every two years','Every month','Every 20 years','Never','To keep certification.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your two-person team would use the 15-minute review and the 5-minute presentation to earn the most points on the Round Two rating sheet.',rubric:[['Review strategy',['split','timeline','history','differential','regroup','notes']],['Content items',['patient information','chief complaint','past medical history','social','family','chronological','diagnosis','treatment plan']],['Treatment plan detail',['medications','procedures','monitoring','follow-up','referral','education','prevention']],['Delivery and teamwork',['both','equal','summary','fillers','time','eye contact']]],model:'During the 15-minute review, we divide the case file: one partner builds a chronological timeline of the clinical course, recording dates, symptoms, exam findings, test results and treatments, while the other gathers the patient information—demographics, chief complaint, past medical history, medications, allergies and social and family history—and starts a differential diagnosis. After about eight minutes we regroup, compare the evidence and agree on the most likely diagnosis, noting one or two alternatives we ruled out. Then we build a complete treatment plan together, because it is worth 20 points: medications, procedures or surgery if needed, monitoring and follow-up, referrals to specialists, patient education, lifestyle changes and prevention. In the five-minute presentation, one partner introduces the team and patient, presents the subjective and objective information and walks through the clinical course in chronological order. The other states the diagnosis with the key supporting evidence, presents the treatment plan and gives a summary of the significant points. We split the time equally to earn team-participation points, speak clearly with varied pace, keep eye contact, avoid fillers like “um” and watch for the one-minute warning so we finish on time.'},sources:[RULES,PAEA,AAPA,NCCPA,BLS_PA]};

export const A2={title:'Patient assessment: history, physical exam and documentation',hook:'Experienced clinicians say that most diagnoses can be made from the history alone. Asking the right questions in the right order—and examining the patient systematically—turns a vague complaint like “my stomach hurts” into a short list of likely causes. Patient assessment is 12% of the test, and it is exactly what the Round Two rating sheet asks you to present first.',objectives:['Take a complete history using OLDCARTS for the history of present illness.','Distinguish subjective and objective data and organize them in a SOAP note.','Describe the four physical exam techniques and the normal adult vital signs.','Perform a review of systems and identify red-flag findings.'],sections:[
['The medical history',[
'A complete history includes **identifying data** (age, sex, occupation), the **chief complaint** (in the patient’s words), the **history of present illness (HPI)**, **past medical history** (illnesses, hospitalizations, surgeries, immunizations), **medications** (prescription, over-the-counter, supplements), **allergies** (and the reaction), **family history** (conditions in parents, siblings and children), **social history** (tobacco, alcohol, drugs, occupation, living situation, sexual history, diet, exercise) and the **review of systems (ROS)**.',
'Use open-ended questions first (“Tell me about the pain”), then focused questions. Listen without interrupting, use plain language and respect privacy. Teens are interviewed partly alone when possible. An interpreter should be used when language differs. The history must be **organized** so others can follow it—the same skill the case presentation demands.',
order('Order of a complete history',['Identifying data and chief complaint','History of present illness','Past medical history','Medications and allergies','Family history','Social history','Review of systems'])]],
['The history of present illness: OLDCARTS',[
'The HPI describes the main problem in detail. A common memory aid is **OLDCARTS**: **O**nset (when and how it started), **L**ocation, **D**uration, **C**haracter (sharp, dull, burning, pressure), **A**ggravating and **A**lleviating factors, **R**adiation (does it spread?), **T**iming (constant or intermittent, time of day) and **S**everity (0–10 scale). Another aid, **OPQRST**, covers Onset, Provocation/palliation, Quality, Region/radiation, Severity and Time.',
'Also ask about **associated symptoms** (fever, nausea, shortness of breath), what the patient has tried and what they think is happening. **Pertinent positives** are symptoms present that support a diagnosis; **pertinent negatives** are absent symptoms that help rule out others (“no fever, no blood in stool”).',
match('OLDCARTS',[['Onset','“It started suddenly two hours ago.”'],['Location','“Right lower abdomen.”'],['Duration','“It has lasted 6 hours.”'],['Character','“Sharp and stabbing.”'],['Aggravating/alleviating','“Worse when I walk; better lying still.”'],['Radiation','“It moves to my back.”'],['Timing','“Constant since this morning.”'],['Severity','“8 out of 10.”']])]],
['Subjective, objective and SOAP',[
'**Subjective** data come from the patient (or family): symptoms, history and feelings—“I feel dizzy.” **Objective** data are observed or measured: vital signs, exam findings, lab and imaging results—“BP 88/56, skin pale and cool.” The rating sheet asks that patient information include **both**. Clinicians document in a **SOAP note**: **S**ubjective, **O**bjective, **A**ssessment (diagnosis or differential) and **P**lan (tests, treatments, education, follow-up).',
'A **problem list** summarizes active diagnoses. In a case presentation, you essentially deliver an oral SOAP note: the patient’s story and data first, then your assessment and plan.',
"Case files often include **nursing notes**, **medication administration records**, consult notes and discharge summaries. Read the **dates and times** on each document so you can rebuild the clinical course in order, and note who recorded each finding. Changes between documents—a new fever, a rising creatinine, a medication stopped—are often the turning points of the case.",
sort('Subjective or objective?',['Subjective','Objective'],[['“My chest feels tight.”',0],['Heart rate 118',1],['Wheezes heard in both lungs',1],['Patient reports nausea',0],['Hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL',1],['“I have been very tired.”',0]])]],
['Physical examination techniques',[
'The four classic techniques are **inspection** (looking—color, symmetry, swelling, rashes, breathing effort), **palpation** (feeling—tenderness, masses, temperature, pulses), **percussion** (tapping to hear dullness over fluid or solid organs and resonance over air-filled lung) and **auscultation** (listening with a stethoscope to the heart, lungs, bowel sounds and blood vessels). The usual order is inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation—**except the abdomen**, where auscultation comes **before** palpation and percussion so bowel sounds are not altered.',
'A general survey notes appearance, distress and mental status. Specific exam findings point to diagnoses: **McBurney’s point** tenderness (appendicitis), **Murphy’s sign** (cholecystitis), **crackles** (fluid in alveoli, as in pneumonia or heart failure), **wheezes** (narrowed airways), **jugular venous distension** (heart failure) and **pitting edema**.',
order('Abdominal exam order',['Inspection','Auscultation','Percussion','Palpation']),
match('Finding and meaning',[['Crackles','Fluid in the alveoli'],['Wheezes','Narrowed airways'],['Murphy’s sign','Gallbladder inflammation'],['McBurney’s point tenderness','Appendicitis'],['Jugular venous distension','Possible heart failure'],['Dullness on lung percussion','Fluid or consolidation']])]],
['Vital signs and the review of systems',[
'Usual adult resting ranges: **temperature** about 97.8–99.1 °F (36.5–37.3 °C); **pulse** 60–100 beats per minute; **respirations** 12–20 per minute; **blood pressure** below 120/80 mm Hg; **oxygen saturation** about 95–100% on room air. **Pain** is often called the fifth vital sign. **Fever** with a fast heart rate and low blood pressure can signal **sepsis**; a high blood pressure with a slow pulse and irregular breathing after head injury can signal rising intracranial pressure.',
'The **review of systems** asks about each body system from head to toe—general (fever, weight loss, fatigue), skin, HEENT, cardiovascular, respiratory, GI, genitourinary, musculoskeletal, neurologic, psychiatric, endocrine and hematologic—to uncover problems the patient did not mention. **Red flags** that need urgent attention include chest pain with sweating, sudden severe headache, new weakness or speech trouble, difficulty breathing, coughing or vomiting blood, black stools, unintentional weight loss and suicidal thoughts.',
{t:'w',kind:'vitalsreport',title:'Report or record?',prompt:'Decide which adult readings fall within the usual teaching ranges and which you would flag in your case presentation.',person:'patient',reportTo:'supervising physician'}]],
['Practice: build the patient information',[
kase('Taking the history',{a:{text:'A 34-year-old says, “My belly hurts.” What is your best next question? (Case invented for practice.)',choices:[['“Can you tell me more about when it started and where you feel it?”','b','Correct. Open-ended OLDCARTS questions first.',true],['“It’s probably gas, right?”','b','Leading questions bias the history.',false]]},b:{text:'She says the pain began around her navel last night and moved to the right lower abdomen, with nausea and no diarrhea. Which items are pertinent negatives?',choices:[['No diarrhea','c','Correct. An absent symptom that helps narrow the differential.',true],['Pain moved to the right lower abdomen','c','That is a pertinent positive.',false]]},c:{text:'You document onset, location, migration, associated symptoms and negatives—a strong patient-information section.',end:true}})]],
["Communicating with patients and the team",["Assessment depends on trust. Introduce yourself and your role, sit at eye level, use **plain language** and **teach-back** (“Can you tell me in your own words how you’ll take this medicine?”), and respect cultural beliefs and health literacy. Use a **trained interpreter** rather than family members, especially children. Sensitive topics—sexual history, substance use, intimate partner violence—are asked privately and without judgment, explaining that you ask all patients the same questions.","Clinicians share information with the team using structured formats such as **SBAR** (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation). The Round Two presentation works the same way: give the judges a clear, organized story they can follow without reading the file. Use medical terms correctly but define uncommon ones, and keep subjective statements in the patient’s words when they matter.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"SBAR","pairs":[["Situation","What is happening now"],["Background","Relevant history and context"],["Assessment","What you think the problem is"],["Recommendation","What you suggest should happen next"],["Teach-back","Patient explains instructions in their own words"],["Interpreter","Trained professional, not a family member"]]}]]
],takeaways:['A complete history: identifying data, chief complaint, HPI (OLDCARTS), past medical history, medications, allergies, family and social history and review of systems.','Subjective data come from the patient and objective data are measured; SOAP organizes subjective, objective, assessment and plan.','Exam: inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation (abdomen: inspect, auscultate, percuss, palpate); know adult vital-sign ranges and red-flag symptoms.'],terms:[['History of present illness','A detailed description of the current problem.'],['Pertinent negative','An absent symptom that helps rule out a diagnosis.'],['Review of systems','A head-to-toe checklist of symptoms by body system.']],mcqs:[q('In OLDCARTS, what does R stand for?','Radiation','Rate','Rhythm','Remedies','Does the pain spread?'),q('Which is objective data?','Blood pressure 150/94','“I feel anxious.”','“The pain is sharp.”','“I can’t sleep.”','Measured.'),q('In the abdominal exam, auscultation comes:','Before palpation and percussion','Last','It is skipped','After palpation','So bowel sounds are not altered.'),q('Crackles heard in the lungs suggest:','Fluid in the alveoli','Narrowed airways only','A normal exam','Fractured ribs','As in pneumonia or heart failure.'),q('What is the normal adult resting pulse range?','60–100','40–60','100–140','12–20','12–20 is respirations.'),q('Family history covers:','Health conditions of blood relatives','The patient’s own surgeries','Current medications','Tobacco use','Social history covers tobacco.'),q('What does the A in SOAP stand for?','Assessment','Allergies','Activity','Auscultation','The diagnosis or differential.'),q('Which is a red-flag symptom?','Sudden severe headache','Mild seasonal sneezing','Occasional dry skin','A small paper cut','Needs urgent evaluation.')],frq:{prompt:'A 58-year-old man reports chest discomfort. Describe the history questions you would ask (including OLDCARTS), the physical exam you would perform and how you would organize the subjective and objective data in your case presentation.',rubric:[['OLDCARTS',['onset','location','duration','character','aggravating','radiation','timing','severity']],['Other history',['past medical','medications','allergies','family','social','smoking','review of systems']],['Physical exam',['vital signs','inspection','palpation','auscultation','heart','lungs','jvd','edema']],['Organization',['subjective','objective','soap','assessment','plan','chronological']]],model:'I begin with an open-ended question and then use OLDCARTS: when the discomfort began and what he was doing (onset), where it is (location), how long it lasts (duration), whether it feels like pressure, sharp or burning (character), what makes it worse or better, such as exertion or rest (aggravating and alleviating), whether it spreads to the arm, jaw or back (radiation), whether it is constant or comes and goes (timing) and how severe it is on a 0–10 scale. I ask about associated symptoms such as sweating, nausea and shortness of breath. Then I take his past medical history—hypertension, diabetes, high cholesterol, prior heart disease—medications, allergies, family history of early heart disease and social history, including smoking, alcohol, drug use and activity, followed by a review of systems. The exam starts with vital signs, including oxygen saturation, and a general survey for distress or sweating, then inspection, palpation of the chest wall and pulses, and auscultation of the heart and lungs, checking for murmurs, crackles, jugular venous distension and leg edema. In the presentation I give the subjective data first—his story and history—then the objective data—vital signs, exam, ECG and labs—before the assessment and plan, in chronological order like an oral SOAP note.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_EXAM,MEDLINE_LABS]};

export const A3={title:'Diagnostic reasoning, laboratory tests and imaging',hook:'A fever and cough could be a cold, influenza, pneumonia, COVID-19, tuberculosis or lung cancer. Diagnosis is a process: build a differential, estimate which conditions are most likely and most dangerous, then use tests to rule in or rule out. Diagnosis is the largest single area of the test (14%) and worth 20 points in Round Two.',objectives:['Build and narrow a differential diagnosis using history, exam and probability.','Interpret sensitivity, specificity and predictive value.','Recognize normal values and patterns for common laboratory tests.','Choose appropriate imaging and avoid common reasoning errors.'],sections:[
['Building a differential diagnosis',[
'A **differential diagnosis** lists conditions that could explain the findings, ranked by **likelihood** and by **danger**. Start with the chief complaint and key features, then think by **organ system**, **anatomy** (what lies under the painful area?) or **mechanism** (infection, inflammation, vascular, neoplasm, trauma, metabolic, congenital, drug-related). Always consider the **“can’t-miss” diagnoses**—conditions that are dangerous if overlooked, such as heart attack, pulmonary embolism, aortic dissection, meningitis, ectopic pregnancy and sepsis.',
'**Illness scripts** describe a disease’s typical patient, timeline and features (“an older smoker with weight loss and a chronic cough” suggests lung cancer). Compare the case with each script and look for **discriminating features** that favor one diagnosis over another. The final step is choosing the **leading diagnosis**—the one that best explains all the findings.',
order('Diagnostic reasoning steps',['Identify the chief complaint and key features','Generate a broad differential','Rank by likelihood and danger','Gather discriminating history, exam and tests','Revise the differential','Choose the leading diagnosis and plan'])]],
['Test accuracy',[
'**Sensitivity** is the proportion of people **with** the disease who test **positive**. A highly sensitive test rarely misses disease, so a **negative** result helps **rule out** (**SnNout**). **Specificity** is the proportion of people **without** the disease who test **negative**. A highly specific test rarely gives false positives, so a **positive** result helps **rule in** (**SpPin**). **Predictive values** depend on how common the disease is: in a low-risk population, even a good test produces many false positives.',
'Example: the **D-dimer** is sensitive but not specific for blood clots—a normal result in a low-risk patient makes a pulmonary embolism unlikely, but a high result has many causes (surgery, pregnancy, infection). Clinicians combine **pretest probability** (often with scores such as the Wells criteria) with test results.',
{t:'example',title:'Calculate sensitivity and specificity',prompt:'A test is used on 100 people with a disease and 100 without it. It is positive in 90 of those with disease and negative in 80 of those without it. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Sensitivity = true positives ÷ all with disease = 90 ÷ 100 = 90%','Specificity = true negatives ÷ all without disease = 80 ÷ 100 = 80%','False positives = 100 − 80 = 20'],answer:'Sensitivity 90%, specificity 80%.'},
match('Test accuracy terms',[['Sensitivity','True positives among those with disease'],['Specificity','True negatives among those without disease'],['SnNout','Sensitive test, negative result rules out'],['SpPin','Specific test, positive result rules in'],['False positive','Positive test without disease'],['Pretest probability','Likelihood of disease before testing']])]],
['Common laboratory tests',[
'A **complete blood count (CBC)** measures white blood cells (about **4,500–11,000/µL**; high in infection or leukemia), hemoglobin (about **12–16 g/dL** in women, **13.5–17.5** in men; low in anemia), MCV (red cell size; low in iron deficiency, high in B₁₂ or folate deficiency) and platelets (about **150,000–450,000/µL**). The **basic or comprehensive metabolic panel** measures sodium (135–145 mEq/L), potassium (3.5–5.0), glucose (fasting 70–99 mg/dL), BUN and **creatinine** (kidney function), calcium and—on the comprehensive panel—**liver enzymes** (ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase), bilirubin and albumin.',
'Other common tests: **lipid panel**; **hemoglobin A1c** (≥6.5% indicates diabetes); **TSH** (high in hypothyroidism, low in hyperthyroidism); **troponin** (heart muscle injury); **BNP** (heart failure); **lipase** (pancreatitis); **urinalysis** (nitrites and leukocyte esterase in UTI; protein in kidney disease); **hCG** (pregnancy); **cultures** (identify bacteria and antibiotic sensitivities); and **antibody serology tests**, which detect antibodies to infections such as hepatitis and HIV and to the body’s own tissues in **autoimmune disorders**—they are not used to diagnose conditions such as osteoporosis.',
match('Lab result → likely meaning',[['Elevated troponin','Heart muscle injury'],['High TSH, low T4','Hypothyroidism'],['Lipase more than 3× normal','Acute pancreatitis'],['Low hemoglobin and low MCV','Iron-deficiency anemia'],['Nitrite-positive urine','Bacterial urinary tract infection'],['A1c 7.2%','Diabetes'],['Elevated BNP','Heart failure'],['High creatinine','Reduced kidney function']])]],
['Imaging and other studies',[
'**X-rays** show bones, lungs (pneumonia, effusion) and some bowel problems. **CT scans** give detailed cross-sections—**noncontrast head CT** for suspected stroke or bleeding, CT abdomen for appendicitis, **CT pulmonary angiography** for pulmonary embolism. **Ultrasound** uses no radiation and is preferred for the **gallbladder**, pregnancy, pelvic organs, DVT and in children. **MRI** gives excellent soft-tissue detail of the brain, spinal cord, joints and ligaments.',
'Other studies include the **ECG** (rhythm, ischemia, infarction), **echocardiogram** (heart structure and pumping), **spirometry** (asthma and COPD), **endoscopy** and **colonoscopy** (view and biopsy the GI tract), **lumbar puncture** (meningitis, bleeding), **polysomnography** (sleep apnea) and **biopsy**, which is required to diagnose most cancers.',
sort('Best first imaging?',['Ultrasound','CT','MRI'],[['Right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals',0],['Sudden worst headache of life',1],['Suspected DVT in the calf',0],['Suspected torn knee ligament',2],['Suspected appendicitis in an adult',1],['Suspected spinal cord compression',2]])]],
['Avoiding reasoning errors',[
'**Anchoring bias** means sticking with the first diagnosis even when new data disagree. **Premature closure** means stopping the search once one diagnosis seems to fit. **Availability bias** means favoring diagnoses you have seen recently. **Confirmation bias** means noticing only data that support your idea. Good clinicians use **diagnostic time-outs**: “What else could this be? What doesn’t fit? What can’t I miss?”',
'In the case challenge, check that your diagnosis explains **every** major finding in the documents—vital signs, exam, labs and imaging. If one finding does not fit, consider a second diagnosis or a complication. State the evidence clearly so judges can follow your reasoning.',
kase('Diagnostic time-out',{a:{text:'A 28-year-old woman has sudden shortness of breath and a fast heart rate. She recently started an estrogen-containing birth control pill and took a 10-hour flight. A teammate says, “It’s anxiety.” What should you do? (Case invented for practice.)',choices:[['Ask what can’t be missed—pulmonary embolism—and look for supporting evidence','b','Correct. Avoid premature closure.',true],['Agree; she is young and healthy','b','Risk factors point to a dangerous diagnosis.',false]]},b:{text:'Her D-dimer is elevated and CT pulmonary angiography shows a clot. What is the diagnosis?',choices:[['Pulmonary embolism','c','Correct. Imaging confirms it.',true],['Panic disorder','c','The CT finding confirms a clot.',false]]},c:{text:'You present the diagnosis with its risk factors and evidence, and mention anxiety as a ruled-out alternative.',end:true}})]],
["From data to diagnosis",["A useful habit is the **problem representation**: a one-sentence summary using precise terms. “A 45-year-old woman with obesity presents with **acute**, **postprandial** RUQ pain, fever and a positive Murphy’s sign” points directly to cholecystitis. Words such as acute versus chronic, unilateral versus bilateral and episodic versus progressive sharpen the differential. Write this sentence early in the 15-minute review; it often becomes the opening line of your diagnosis section.",{"t":"check","q":"Which problem representation is most useful?","options":["A 70-year-old smoker with 3 months of progressive dyspnea, weight loss and hemoptysis","An older patient who feels sick","A patient with a cough","Someone with many problems"],"why":"Precise descriptors narrow the differential."}]]
],takeaways:['Build a differential by system, anatomy or mechanism; rank by likelihood and danger; always consider can’t-miss diagnoses.','Sensitivity rules out when negative (SnNout); specificity rules in when positive (SpPin); interpret tests with pretest probability.','Know CBC, metabolic panel, troponin, BNP, lipase, A1c, TSH, urinalysis and serology patterns; choose ultrasound, CT or MRI wisely; avoid anchoring and premature closure.'],terms:[['Sensitivity','The proportion of people with a disease who test positive.'],['Specificity','The proportion of people without a disease who test negative.'],['Anchoring bias','Sticking with a first impression despite contrary evidence.']],mcqs:[q('Which of these diseases are antibody serology tests NOT used to detect?','Osteoporosis','Hepatitis','Severe autoimmune disorders','HIV','HOSA sample question (adapted).'),q('A highly sensitive test with a negative result helps:','Rule out disease','Rule in disease','Nothing','Prove cancer','SnNout.'),q('Which imaging is preferred first for suspected gallstones?','Ultrasound','MRI','Bone scan','PET scan','No radiation, excellent for the gallbladder.'),q('An elevated troponin suggests:','Heart muscle injury','Pancreatitis','Hypothyroidism','Kidney stones','Myocardial damage.'),q('Low hemoglobin with a low MCV suggests:','Iron-deficiency anemia','B₁₂ deficiency','Polycythemia','Dehydration','Small red cells.'),q('Sticking with the first diagnosis despite new evidence is:','Anchoring bias','Specificity','Sensitivity','Differential diagnosis','A common error.'),q('Which test is required to diagnose most cancers?','Biopsy','CBC','Urinalysis','ECG','Tissue diagnosis.'),q('An A1c of 6.8% indicates:','Diabetes','Normal glucose','Hypoglycemia','Anemia','≥6.5%.')],frq:{prompt:'A 45-year-old woman presents with right upper abdominal pain after meals. Explain how you would build and narrow a differential diagnosis, which laboratory and imaging tests you would order and how you would avoid common reasoning errors.',rubric:[['Differential',['cholecystitis','gallstones','hepatitis','pancreatitis','peptic ulcer','myocardial','pneumonia']],['History and exam',['fatty','murphy','fever','jaundice','radiation','shoulder']],['Tests',['cbc','liver','lipase','ultrasound','bilirubin','troponin','ecg']],['Reasoning',['likelihood','danger','can’t miss','anchoring','premature closure','explains all findings']]],model:'I start with the anatomy under the right upper quadrant—gallbladder, liver, duodenum, pancreas, right lung base and right kidney—and build a differential that includes biliary colic or acute cholecystitis from gallstones, hepatitis, pancreatitis, peptic ulcer disease, right lower-lobe pneumonia and kidney stones, plus can’t-miss conditions such as an atypical heart attack. Discriminating history includes pain after fatty meals and radiation to the right shoulder (favoring gallbladder disease), fever and jaundice (favoring cholecystitis or bile duct stones), alcohol use, cough and urinary symptoms. On exam, a positive Murphy’s sign supports cholecystitis. I would order a CBC for an elevated white count, liver enzymes and bilirubin, lipase for pancreatitis, a urinalysis and pregnancy test as appropriate, and an ECG with troponin if any cardiac risk exists, with a right upper quadrant ultrasound as the first imaging test because it detects gallstones and gallbladder wall thickening without radiation. I would rank the diagnoses by likelihood and danger, check that my leading diagnosis explains every finding and use a diagnostic time-out—asking what else it could be and what does not fit—to avoid anchoring and premature closure.'},sources:[RULES,MEDLINE_LABS,MEDLINE_SERO,MEDLINE_CBC]};

export const A4={title:'Disease process, terminology and cancer',hook:'Every disease follows a process: a cause, a mechanism, changes in tissues and signs and symptoms. Understanding that process lets you predict a patient’s course and choose treatment. Cancer—uncontrolled cell growth—is one of the most important examples. Disease process and terminology, including cancer and neoplasms, is 10% of the test.',objectives:['Use medical word parts and disease-process vocabulary accurately.','Describe inflammation, infection and immune responses.','Compare benign and malignant neoplasms and explain grading and TNM staging.','Summarize cancer screening recommendations and treatment approaches.'],sections:[
['Medical terminology',[
'Medical terms combine **prefixes**, **roots** and **suffixes**. Common suffixes: **-itis** (inflammation), **-osis** (abnormal condition), **-emia** (blood condition), **-algia** (pain), **-ectomy** (removal), **-otomy** (incision), **-ostomy** (new opening), **-plasty** (surgical repair), **-oma** (tumor), **-pathy** (disease) and **-megaly** (enlargement). Common prefixes: **hyper-** (above), **hypo-** (below), **brady-** (slow), **tachy-** (fast), **dys-** (difficult or abnormal), **a-/an-** (without) and **poly-** (many).',
'Disease vocabulary: **etiology** (cause), **pathogenesis** (how it develops), **signs** (objective) and **symptoms** (subjective), **acute** (sudden, short) versus **chronic** (long-lasting), **idiopathic** (unknown cause), **iatrogenic** (caused by treatment), **prognosis** (expected outcome), **morbidity** (illness) and **mortality** (death), **incidence** (new cases) and **prevalence** (all existing cases). A **uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP)** is surgical repair (-plasty) of the uvula, palate and pharynx—used to treat **obstructive sleep apnea**.',
{t:'w',kind:'termlab',title:'Take apart clinical terms',words:[{word:'cholecystitis',parts:['','chole-cyst','-itis'],meanings:['no prefix','gallbladder (bile sac)','inflammation'],meaning:'Inflammation of the gallbladder.'},{word:'hepatomegaly',parts:['','hepat/o','-megaly'],meanings:['no prefix','liver','enlargement'],meaning:'Enlargement of the liver.'},{word:'tachycardia',parts:['tachy-','cardi','-ia'],meanings:['fast','heart','condition'],meaning:'A fast heart rate, over 100 beats per minute in adults.'},{word:'nephrectomy',parts:['','nephr','-ectomy'],meanings:['no prefix','kidney','surgical removal'],meaning:'Surgical removal of a kidney.'}]},
match('Disease-process terms',[['Etiology','Cause of a disease'],['Idiopathic','Unknown cause'],['Iatrogenic','Caused by medical treatment'],['Prognosis','Expected outcome'],['Incidence','New cases in a period'],['Prevalence','All existing cases at a time']])]],
['Inflammation, infection and immunity',[
'**Inflammation** is the body’s response to injury or infection. Its cardinal signs are **redness, heat, swelling, pain and loss of function**. **Acute** inflammation brings neutrophils and fluid; **chronic** inflammation (as in rheumatoid arthritis or inflammatory bowel disease) involves lymphocytes and macrophages and can damage tissue over time. Markers include white blood cell count, **C-reactive protein (CRP)** and **erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)**.',
"Cells also adapt to stress: **hypertrophy** (larger cells, as in the heart with hypertension), **hyperplasia** (more cells), **atrophy** (smaller cells with disuse) and **metaplasia** (one cell type replaced by another, as in Barrett’s esophagus from acid reflux). **Dysplasia** is disordered growth that can progress to cancer.",
'**Infections** are caused by bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites. **Innate immunity** (skin, neutrophils, fever) responds quickly; **adaptive immunity** uses **B cells** (antibodies) and **T cells**, and it remembers past exposures—the basis of vaccines. **Autoimmune** diseases (type 1 diabetes, lupus, rheumatoid arthritis, Graves’ disease) occur when the immune system attacks the body. **Hypersensitivity** reactions include allergies and anaphylaxis. **Sepsis** is a life-threatening dysregulated response to infection that can cause organ failure.',
sort('Innate or adaptive immunity?',['Innate','Adaptive'],[['Intact skin barrier',0],['Fever',0],['Antibodies made by B cells',1],['Neutrophils engulfing bacteria',0],['Memory T cells after a vaccine',1],['Long-lasting immunity to measles',1]])]],
['Neoplasms: benign and malignant',[
'A **neoplasm** (“new growth”) or tumor is an abnormal mass of cells. **Benign** tumors grow slowly, are well defined and often **encapsulated**, look like normal tissue and **do not spread** (though they can cause problems by pressing on organs). **Malignant** tumors (cancers) grow faster, **invade** nearby tissue and can **metastasize**—spread through blood or lymph to distant sites such as the lungs, liver, bones and brain. Cancer cells often look abnormal (**anaplasia**).',
'Cancers are named by tissue of origin: **carcinomas** arise from epithelial cells (most common—skin, lung, breast, colon, prostate); **sarcomas** from connective tissue such as bone and muscle; **leukemias** from blood-forming cells in bone marrow; **lymphomas** from lymphocytes in lymph nodes; **melanoma** from pigment cells. Risk factors include **tobacco** (the leading preventable cause), alcohol, UV exposure, obesity, certain infections (HPV, hepatitis B and C, *H. pylori*), radiation, age and inherited mutations such as **BRCA1/2**.',
sort('Benign or malignant?',['Benign','Malignant'],[['Encapsulated and slow-growing',0],['Invades surrounding tissue',1],['Can metastasize',1],['Cells resemble normal tissue',0],['Lipoma (fatty tumor)',0],['Adenocarcinoma of the colon',1]])]],
['Grading, staging and treatment',[
'**Grade** describes how abnormal cancer cells look under the microscope (low-grade cells look more normal and grow slowly; high-grade cells look very abnormal and grow fast). **Stage** describes how far the cancer has spread. The **TNM** system records **T**umor size and extent, **N**ode involvement and **M**etastasis; stages run from **0** (in situ) to **IV** (distant metastasis). Stage is the most important factor for prognosis and treatment choice.',
'Treatments include **surgery** (removal), **radiation therapy**, **chemotherapy** (drugs that kill rapidly dividing cells), **targeted therapy**, **immunotherapy** (helping the immune system attack cancer), **hormone therapy** (for some breast and prostate cancers) and **stem cell transplant**. Care also includes managing side effects (nausea, low blood counts, infection risk), nutrition, pain control, psychological support and **palliative care**, which improves quality of life at any stage.',
"Cancer emergencies can appear in case files: **neutropenic fever** (fever with a very low neutrophil count after chemotherapy) requires immediate IV antibiotics; **spinal cord compression** from metastases causes back pain and leg weakness; **tumor lysis syndrome** after treatment releases potassium, phosphate and uric acid; and **hypercalcemia** causes confusion, constipation and dehydration. Recognizing them shows judges that your plan covers complications, not just the cancer itself.",
match('TNM and treatment',[['T','Size and extent of the primary tumor'],['N','Spread to lymph nodes'],['M','Distant metastasis'],['Stage IV','Cancer has spread to distant organs'],['Immunotherapy','Helps the immune system attack cancer'],['Palliative care','Relieves symptoms and improves quality of life']])]],
['Cancer screening',[
'Screening finds cancer early in people without symptoms. Current **USPSTF** recommendations include: **colorectal** cancer screening for adults **45–75** (colonoscopy, stool tests or other options); **breast** cancer screening with **mammography every 2 years for women 40–74**; **cervical** cancer screening from **21–65** (Pap tests every 3 years, or from age 30 HPV testing every 5 years); and **lung** cancer screening with annual **low-dose CT** for adults **50–80** with a **20 pack-year** smoking history who smoke now or quit within the past 15 years. **Prostate** cancer screening with **PSA** for men 55–69 is an individual decision after discussing benefits and harms.',
'Warning signs that need evaluation include unexplained **weight loss**, a lump, a changing mole, persistent cough or hoarseness, blood in the stool or urine, abnormal bleeding, difficulty swallowing and a sore that does not heal. In the case challenge, a cancer diagnosis should include the tissue type and stage when given, and the treatment plan should address the cancer, symptoms and support.',
sort('Recommended screening?',['Recommended by USPSTF for this person','Not routinely recommended'],[['Colonoscopy for a 50-year-old',0],['Mammogram every 2 years for a 55-year-old woman',0],['Low-dose CT for a 60-year-old with 30 pack-years who quit 5 years ago',0],['Lung CT for a 25-year-old non-smoker',1],['Pap test for a 30-year-old',0],['Colonoscopy screening for a healthy 20-year-old',1]])]],
['Practice: interpret a pathology report',[
kase('Reading a cancer case',{a:{text:'A pathology report reads: “Invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast, grade 3, tumor 2.5 cm, 2 of 12 lymph nodes positive, no distant metastasis.” Which tissue type is this? (Case invented for practice.)',choices:[['Carcinoma (epithelial)','b','Correct. Ductal carcinoma arises from duct epithelium.',true],['Sarcoma','b','Sarcomas arise from connective tissue.',false]]},b:{text:'What do “2 of 12 lymph nodes positive” and “no distant metastasis” tell you?',choices:[['N is positive (nodal spread) and M0','c','Correct. The cancer has reached nodes but not distant organs.',true],['It is benign','c','Invasion and nodal spread mean malignant.',false]]},c:{text:'You present the cancer type, grade and TNM findings, then a plan of surgery, systemic therapy and support.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Use word parts (-itis, -osis, -ectomy, -plasty, -oma, hyper-, tachy-) and process terms (etiology, idiopathic, iatrogenic, prognosis, incidence, prevalence); UPPP treats sleep apnea.','Inflammation shows redness, heat, swelling, pain and loss of function; innate and adaptive immunity; autoimmunity and sepsis.','Benign tumors stay local; malignant tumors invade and metastasize; grade = cell appearance, TNM stage = spread; screen colorectal 45–75, breast 40–74 biennially, cervical 21–65, lung LDCT 50–80 with 20 pack-years.'],terms:[['Neoplasm','An abnormal new growth of cells.'],['Metastasis','Spread of cancer to a distant part of the body.'],['TNM staging','A system describing tumor size, node involvement and metastasis.']],mcqs:[q('A patient might have a uvulopalatopharyngoplasty (UPPP) to treat what condition?','Sleep apnea','Impacted molar','Cavity','Canker sores','HOSA sample question.'),q('The suffix -itis means:','Inflammation','Removal','Tumor','Pain','Appendicitis.'),q('A cancer arising from epithelial cells is a:','Carcinoma','Sarcoma','Leukemia','Lymphoma','Most common type.'),q('In TNM staging, N refers to:','Lymph node involvement','Number of tumors','Necrosis','Nerve invasion','Node.'),q('A condition caused by medical treatment is:','Iatrogenic','Idiopathic','Congenital','Acute','From treatment.'),q('At what age does USPSTF recommend starting colorectal cancer screening for average-risk adults?','45','30','60','21','Through 75.'),q('Which feature describes malignant tumors?','They can metastasize','They are always encapsulated','They never grow','They look like normal tissue','Invasion and spread.'),q('Prevalence means:','All existing cases at a given time','New cases during a period','Deaths from a disease','The cause of disease','Incidence counts new cases.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare benign and malignant neoplasms, explain how cancers are graded and staged, and describe a complete treatment plan for a patient with stage III colon cancer.',rubric:[['Benign vs malignant',['benign','encapsulated','local','malignant','invade','metastasize']],['Grade and stage',['grade','cells','tnm','tumor','node','metastasis','stage']],['Treatment',['surgery','chemotherapy','radiation','targeted','immunotherapy']],['Support and follow-up',['side effects','nutrition','pain','palliative','surveillance','colonoscopy','education']]],model:'Benign neoplasms usually grow slowly, have clear borders and are often encapsulated, resemble normal tissue and do not invade or spread, although they can cause symptoms by pressure. Malignant neoplasms grow faster, invade surrounding tissue and can metastasize through blood or lymph to distant organs. Grade describes how abnormal the cancer cells look under a microscope—high-grade cells look very abnormal and tend to grow quickly. Stage describes extent using the TNM system: T for the size and depth of the primary tumor, N for spread to lymph nodes and M for distant metastasis, combined into stages 0 to IV. Stage III colon cancer has spread to regional lymph nodes but not to distant organs. A complete treatment plan includes surgical removal of the affected section of colon with nearby lymph nodes, followed by adjuvant chemotherapy to reduce the risk of recurrence; targeted therapy or immunotherapy may be considered depending on tumor markers. Supportive care covers side effects such as nausea and low blood counts, nutrition, pain control, emotional support and palliative care if needed. Follow-up includes surveillance colonoscopies, imaging and blood tests such as CEA, and education about diet, activity and screening for relatives.'},sources:[RULES,NCI_CANCER,NCI_STAGING,USPSTF]};

export const A5={title:'Circulatory and lymphatic disorders',hook:'Heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States, and high blood pressure affects nearly half of adults. Many case-challenge files involve chest pain, shortness of breath, swollen legs or fatigue from anemia. Circulatory and lymphatic disorders are 10% of the test—tied for the largest body-system share.',objectives:['Diagnose and plan treatment for hypertension and acute coronary syndrome.','Describe heart failure, atrial fibrillation, DVT and pulmonary embolism.','Classify anemias using lab patterns.','Recognize lymphatic disorders including lymphadenopathy and lymphoma.'],sections:[
['Hypertension',[
'Blood pressure categories (AHA/ACC): **normal** below 120/80; **elevated** 120–129 and below 80; **stage 1** 130–139 or 80–89; **stage 2** 140/90 or higher; a **hypertensive crisis** is above **180/120**, and with organ damage (chest pain, neurologic symptoms, kidney injury) it is an emergency. Diagnosis needs repeated readings, ideally confirmed with home or ambulatory monitoring. Most hypertension is **primary (essential)**; **secondary** causes include kidney disease, sleep apnea, thyroid disease and certain medications.',
'Treatment includes **lifestyle** changes—the **DASH diet**, less sodium, weight loss, exercise and limiting alcohol—and medications: **thiazide diuretics**, **ACE inhibitors** or **ARBs** (preferred in diabetes and kidney disease) and **calcium channel blockers**. Untreated hypertension causes stroke, heart attack, heart failure, kidney disease and vision loss.',
"**Prevention** ties the circulatory section together. Clinicians estimate a patient’s 10-year cardiovascular risk from age, cholesterol, blood pressure, diabetes and smoking, then decide on **statin** therapy. Lifestyle measures—not smoking, 150 minutes of moderate activity a week, a heart-healthy diet, a healthy weight and good sleep—lower the risk of nearly every disorder in this lesson.",
sort('Blood pressure category',['Normal','Elevated','Stage 1','Stage 2'],[['116/74',0],['126/78',1],['134/84',2],['148/92',3],['138/76',2],['122/70',1]])]],
['Acute coronary syndrome',[
'**Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)** includes **unstable angina**, **NSTEMI** and **STEMI**, usually caused by a ruptured plaque and clot in a coronary artery. Classic symptoms are **chest pressure** radiating to the arm, jaw or back with sweating, nausea and shortness of breath; women, older adults and people with diabetes more often have atypical symptoms such as fatigue or indigestion. Diagnosis uses the **ECG** (ST elevation in STEMI) and **troponin** levels.',
'Treatment is urgent: **aspirin**, nitroglycerin for pain (unless contraindicated), oxygen if saturation is low, anticoagulation and **reperfusion**—**percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI)** with a stent, ideally within **90 minutes** of first medical contact for STEMI, or clot-dissolving drugs if PCI is not available. Long-term plans include **dual antiplatelet therapy**, **beta blockers**, **high-intensity statins**, ACE inhibitors, **cardiac rehabilitation**, smoking cessation and control of blood pressure and diabetes.',
order('STEMI care sequence',['Recognize symptoms; call EMS','12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival','Aspirin and other initial medicines','Emergent PCI to open the artery','Secondary prevention medicines','Cardiac rehabilitation and lifestyle change']),
label('heart','The heart',[['lv','Left ventricle','Most often damaged in a heart attack; weak in heart failure.'],['aorta','Aorta','Main artery to the body.'],['la','Left atrium','Where clots form in atrial fibrillation.'],['ra','Right atrium','Contains the SA node.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Strained by a large pulmonary embolism.'],['mitral','Mitral valve','Between left atrium and ventricle.']])]],
['Heart failure and atrial fibrillation',[
'**Heart failure** means the heart cannot pump enough blood to meet the body’s needs. **Left-sided** failure backs fluid into the lungs—**shortness of breath**, **orthopnea** (breathless lying flat), paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea and **crackles**. **Right-sided** failure causes **jugular venous distension**, **leg edema**, liver congestion and weight gain. **BNP** is elevated, and an **echocardiogram** measures the **ejection fraction** (reduced when 40% or less). Treatment includes **diuretics** for fluid, guideline therapies (ACE inhibitor/ARB/ARNI, beta blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist, SGLT2 inhibitor), salt and fluid limits, daily weights and treating causes.',
'**Atrial fibrillation (AFib)** is an **irregularly irregular** rhythm in which the atria quiver; patients may feel palpitations, fatigue or shortness of breath—or nothing. Blood pools in the left atrium, raising **stroke** risk, which is estimated with the **CHA₂DS₂-VASc** score to decide on **anticoagulation**. Treatment also includes **rate control** (beta blockers, calcium channel blockers) or **rhythm control** (cardioversion, antiarrhythmics, ablation).',
{t:'w',kind:'ecg',title:'Rhythms and artifacts',prompt:'Compare normal sinus rhythm with atrial fibrillation and other rhythms. Which features make AFib “irregularly irregular”?'},
sort('Left- or right-sided heart failure sign?',['Left-sided','Right-sided'],[['Crackles in the lungs',0],['Orthopnea',0],['Jugular venous distension',1],['Leg swelling',1],['Waking up breathless at night',0],['Enlarged, tender liver',1]])]],
['DVT and pulmonary embolism',[
'**Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)** is a clot in a deep vein, usually in the leg, causing **unilateral swelling, pain, warmth and redness**. **Virchow’s triad** describes the causes: **stasis** (immobility, long travel), **endothelial injury** (surgery, trauma) and **hypercoagulability** (cancer, pregnancy, estrogen, inherited disorders). Diagnosis uses **compression ultrasound**, with the D-dimer to help rule out low-risk cases.',
'A **pulmonary embolism (PE)** occurs when a clot travels to the lungs, causing **sudden shortness of breath**, **pleuritic chest pain**, fast heart rate, low oxygen and sometimes collapse. **CT pulmonary angiography** confirms it. Treatment is **anticoagulation** (heparin or a direct oral anticoagulant), with clot-dissolving therapy for massive PE. Prevention includes early walking after surgery, compression devices and preventive anticoagulants in high-risk patients.',
"**Peripheral artery disease** narrows leg arteries, causing **claudication** (calf pain with walking relieved by rest), cool skin, weak pulses and slow-healing wounds; the **ankle-brachial index** confirms it. Treatment includes smoking cessation, supervised walking, antiplatelet therapy and statins. An **abdominal aortic aneurysm** may be found by one-time ultrasound screening in men 65–75 who have ever smoked.",
match('Virchow’s triad and VTE',[['Stasis','Long flight or bed rest'],['Endothelial injury','Recent surgery or trauma'],['Hypercoagulability','Cancer, pregnancy or estrogen'],['DVT test','Compression ultrasound'],['PE test','CT pulmonary angiography'],['Treatment','Anticoagulation']])]],
['Anemia and lymphatic disorders',[
'**Anemia** is a low hemoglobin, causing fatigue, pallor, shortness of breath and a fast heart rate. **Microcytic** anemia (low MCV) is most often **iron deficiency**—from blood loss (heavy periods, GI bleeding) or poor intake—with low **ferritin**; in older adults, look for a GI source such as colon cancer. **Macrocytic** anemia (high MCV) suggests **vitamin B₁₂** (pernicious anemia, vegan diet; may cause nerve symptoms) or **folate** deficiency. **Normocytic** anemia can come from chronic disease, kidney disease or acute bleeding. **Sickle cell disease** causes painful crises and organ damage.',
'The **lymphatic system** returns fluid to the blood and supports immunity. **Lymphadenopathy** (enlarged nodes) is usually from infection, but **painless, firm, persistent** nodes with **B symptoms**—fever, drenching night sweats and weight loss over 10%—suggest **lymphoma**. **Hodgkin lymphoma** has **Reed-Sternberg cells** and often affects young adults; **non-Hodgkin** lymphomas are more common overall. **Lymphedema** is swelling from blocked lymph drainage, for example after lymph-node removal for breast cancer. Leukemias are cancers of blood-forming cells and may cause anemia, infections and bleeding.',
sort('Anemia type by MCV',['Microcytic (low MCV)','Macrocytic (high MCV)'],[['Iron deficiency',0],['Vitamin B₁₂ deficiency',1],['Folate deficiency',1],['Chronic GI blood loss',0],['Pernicious anemia',1],['Heavy menstrual bleeding',0]])]],
['Practice case: chest pain',[
kase('Case: chest pain',{a:{text:'A 62-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes has 45 minutes of crushing chest pressure radiating to his left arm, with sweating. BP 150/90, HR 104. (Case invented for practice.) What is the first test?',choices:[['12-lead ECG within 10 minutes','b','Correct. It identifies STEMI quickly.',true],['Outpatient stress test next week','b','This is an emergency.',false]]},b:{text:'The ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF, and troponin is elevated. What is the diagnosis?',choices:[['Inferior STEMI','c','Correct. ST elevation with elevated troponin.',true],['Stable angina','c','ST elevation and troponin mean infarction.',false]]},c:{text:'Plan: aspirin, anticoagulation, emergent PCI, then dual antiplatelet therapy, beta blocker, high-intensity statin, ACE inhibitor, cardiac rehab and diabetes and BP control.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Hypertension stages: elevated 120–129/<80, stage 1 130–139 or 80–89, stage 2 ≥140/90, crisis >180/120; DASH diet plus thiazides, ACE inhibitors/ARBs or CCBs.','ACS: ECG within 10 minutes, troponin, aspirin and PCI within 90 minutes for STEMI, then DAPT, beta blocker, statin, ACE inhibitor and rehab; heart failure (left = lungs, right = JVD and edema; BNP, ejection fraction); AFib (irregularly irregular, CHA₂DS₂-VASc).','DVT/PE: Virchow’s triad, ultrasound or CT angiography, anticoagulation; anemia by MCV (iron deficiency microcytic, B₁₂/folate macrocytic); painless nodes with B symptoms suggest lymphoma.'],terms:[['STEMI','A heart attack with ST-segment elevation on the ECG.'],['Ejection fraction','The percentage of blood the left ventricle pumps out with each beat.'],['Virchow’s triad','Stasis, endothelial injury and hypercoagulability—the causes of clots.']],mcqs:[q('A blood pressure of 134/84 is:','Stage 1 hypertension','Normal','Elevated','Stage 2 hypertension','130–139 or 80–89.'),q('What is the first test for suspected ACS?','12-lead ECG','Chest MRI','Colonoscopy','Spirometry','Within 10 minutes.'),q('Which lab supports heart failure?','Elevated BNP','Elevated lipase','Low TSH','Positive nitrite','B-type natriuretic peptide.'),q('An irregularly irregular rhythm suggests:','Atrial fibrillation','Normal sinus rhythm','Sinus bradycardia','First-degree block','Quivering atria.'),q('Which test confirms pulmonary embolism?','CT pulmonary angiography','Upper GI series','Urinalysis','Echocardiogram only','Shows the clot.'),q('Iron-deficiency anemia is:','Microcytic','Macrocytic','Normocytic only','Hemolytic only','Low MCV.'),q('Reed-Sternberg cells are found in:','Hodgkin lymphoma','Iron deficiency','Heart failure','Atrial fibrillation','Pathology hallmark.'),q('Which is part of Virchow’s triad?','Venous stasis','High cholesterol','Low potassium','Fever','Plus endothelial injury and hypercoagulability.')],frq:{prompt:'A 70-year-old woman reports three weeks of increasing shortness of breath, needing three pillows to sleep, and swollen ankles. Her BNP is high and an echocardiogram shows an ejection fraction of 30%. Present her diagnosis with supporting evidence and a complete treatment plan.',rubric:[['Diagnosis and evidence',['heart failure','reduced ejection fraction','30%','bnp','orthopnea','edema']],['Exam findings',['crackles','jvd','jugular','weight gain','s3']],['Medications',['diuretic','furosemide','ace inhibitor','arni','beta blocker','spironolactone','sglt2']],['Other plan elements',['sodium','fluid','daily weights','cause','follow-up','education','cardiology','rehab']]],model:'The diagnosis is heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. Evidence includes progressive shortness of breath, orthopnea—she needs three pillows to sleep—bilateral ankle edema, an elevated BNP and an echocardiogram showing an ejection fraction of 30%, which is below the 40% cutoff for reduced ejection fraction. On exam I would expect crackles in the lungs from left-sided failure and jugular venous distension, leg edema and weight gain from fluid retention, and possibly an S3 gallop. I would look for causes such as coronary artery disease, hypertension or valve disease, with an ECG, troponin and possibly coronary evaluation. Treatment includes a loop diuretic such as furosemide to remove fluid while monitoring potassium and kidney function, and guideline-directed medical therapy: an ARNI such as sacubitril/valsartan or an ACE inhibitor or ARB, an evidence-based beta blocker such as metoprolol succinate, a mineralocorticoid antagonist such as spironolactone and an SGLT2 inhibitor. She should limit sodium, follow fluid guidance, weigh herself daily and report a gain of more than 2–3 pounds in a day or 5 pounds in a week, receive vaccines, have cardiology follow-up and attend cardiac rehabilitation, with device therapy considered later if her ejection fraction stays low.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_HBP,MEDLINE_HA,NHLBI_HF,NHLBI_VTE,MEDLINE_ANEMIA,MEDLINE_LYMPH]};
